n religion led to the overthrow of the of the state. […] The church within Venetian borders was essentially a state church to be managed for the benefit of the Republic and the profit of the nobility.” “Far from this recent idealization, medieval and early modern guilds, usually founded and controlled by professional and mercantile élites, were sites of severe social conflicts that contributed to maintaining active discrimination against important sectors of the society: Jews, women, and foreigners.” “As Venice emphasised the respectability and exclusive leadership capabilities of its patrician men, it was clear that Jewish men were not aristocrats.” '}], {'usage.total_tokens': 936, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 836})
